---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/paths';

const pageTitle = "Forward Every N Messages";
const pageDesc = "Deliver only every Nth message each task receives — sampled forwarding for curation, digests and anti-spam, with album and filter aware counting.";
---

<DocsLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDesc}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Features', href: url('/features/') },
    { name: 'Forward Every N Messages', href: url('/features/forward-every-n/') },
  ]}
  prev={{ title: 'Scheduling & Delays', href: url('/features/scheduling-and-delays/') }}
  next={{ title: 'Media & Albums', href: url('/features/media-and-albums/') }}
>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Sometimes you do not want every message a source publishes — you want a sample. Curation channels, digests, anti-spam
      throttling and "keep the destination quiet but alive" all share one need: <strong>forward only every Nth
      message</strong>. AutoTelegramForward gives each task an interval setting, <code>forward_every_n</code>, from 1 to
      1,000. With <code>N = 5</code>, messages 1, 6, 11, 16, … are delivered; everything between them is skipped.
    </p>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 not-prose my-4">
      <div class="text-xs font-mono text-sky-400 mb-1">Configuration Parameter</div>
      <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2"><code>forward_every_n</code> (integer 1–1000, default: 1)</div>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        The default <code>1</code> means "every message": the setting is off, and the task behaves exactly as it did before
        the feature existed — down to not writing a single counter row. Anything above 1 turns the cadence on.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="what-counts">What counts as a message</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Filters decide.</strong> Only messages that pass every configured filter — media type gates, blacklist and
        whitelist keywords, links policy, everything — are counted. A message the filters reject never advances the counter,
        so rejecting ten junk messages does not move your cadence.</li>
      <li><strong>An album is one message.</strong> A media group of any size (2 photos or a 10-item album) consumes exactly
        one slot, and the album as a whole is delivered or skipped together.</li>
      <li><strong>Edits are free.</strong> Edit synchronization of an already-counted message never consumes another slot.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="determinism">Deterministic, restart-proof counting</h2>
    <p>
      The counter lives in the task's own row in the <code>rule_counters</code> table and is advanced by a single atomic
      upsert whose returned sequence decides whether the message owns the current slot. Consequences you can rely on:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Two workers processing a burst cannot both claim the same slot — the database serialises them.</li>
      <li>The count survives restarts, redeploys and backup restores; nothing is recomputed at boot and nothing restarts the cadence silently.</li>
      <li>The counter is per task and per account: one task's cadence is never influenced by another task sharing the same source.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="surfaces">Where you configure it</h2>
    <p>
      The same setting is reachable from every client, and each writes the same number the engine reads:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Telegram bot:</strong> the task panel's <em>Forward Every N Messages</em> row opens the interval screen —
        presets (1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100) plus a typed value for anything up to 1,000, in all ten interface languages.</li>
      <li><strong>Web dashboard:</strong> the task overview's <em>Forward Every N</em> card opens the interval screen with the
        same presets and a number field.</li>
      <li><strong>Mobile apps (iOS & Android):</strong> the task editor carries the field next to the forwarding delay, and
        the per-task settings sheet lists it with the rest of the schema.</li>
      <li><strong>API:</strong> <code>forward_every_n</code> travels with the rule's advanced settings
        (<code>PATCH /api/rules/{'{'}id{'}'}</code> / <code>PATCH /api/rules/{'{'}id{'}'}/settings</code>), clamped to 1–1000 server-side.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="recipes">Two recipes</h2>
    <pre class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400"><code># A news source posts 80 messages a day; you only want the day's shape
forward_every_n = 20        # four posts a day, roughly evenly spaced

# A busy source behaves like spam if forwarded raw
forward_every_n = 5         # every 5th accepted message keeps the feed alive</code></pre>

    <p>
      Interval sampling composes with the rest of the pipeline rather than replacing it: filters still decide what is
      eligible, delays still pace what the slot delivers, cleaners and headers still apply to what is sent, and a failed
      delivery is retried with the normal retry budget — a retry never consumes a second slot.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
